    "summary": "Replace generic div soup with meaningful structural tags understood by search engines.",
    "content": "# Lesson 14: Semantic HTML5 Layout: header, nav, main, section, article, aside, footer\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 14** of the Front-End Developer HTML Track at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Semantic HTML5 Layout: header, nav, main, section, article, aside, footer**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Web Standards\nReplace generic div soup with meaningful structural tags understood by search engines.\n\nHTML is the cornerstone of the World Wide Web. Writing valid, semantic, and well-structured HTML ensures your web pages look great, rank high in search engines, and remain accessible to everyone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Live Code Playground\nTry running and modifying this HTML code in the **Web Dev Lab** playground:\n\n```html\n<header>\n  <nav><a href=\"#home\">Home</a> | <a href=\"#courses\">Courses</a></nav>\n</header>\n<main>\n  <article>\n    <h1>Web Dev Roadmap</h1>\n    <section><h2>HTML Essentials</h2></section>\n  </article>\n  <aside>Related tutorials</aside>\n</main>\n<footer>© 2026 Bargisy eSchool</footer>\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Front-End Best Practice**: Always validate your HTML tags, close all open elements, and use semantic tags (<article>, <section>, <nav>) instead of excessive generic <div> containers!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nComplete this practical challenge:\n1. **Goal**: Draft the semantic HTML structure of a personal tech blog with header, nav, main, article, aside, and footer.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Paste the code into the HTML tab, observe the live rendered preview, and check the interactive results!\n",
