    "content": "# Lesson 20: Front-End Capstone 1: Semantic, Accessible Personal Developer Portfolio\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 20** of the Front-End Developer HTML Track at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Front-End Capstone 1: Semantic, Accessible Personal Developer Portfolio**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Web Standards\nSynthesize all 20 HTML lessons into a complete, accessible, semantic personal portfolio page.\n\nHTML is the cornerstone of the World Wide Web. Writing valid, semantic, and well-structured HTML ensures your web pages look great, rank high in search engines, and remain accessible to everyone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Live Code Playground\nTry running and modifying this HTML code in the **Web Dev Lab** playground:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <title>Mohammed Bargisy | Full-Stack Developer</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <header>\n    <h1>Mohammed Bargisy</h1>\n    <p>Software Engineer & Technical Educator</p>\n    <nav>\n      <ul>\n        <li><a href=\"#about\">About</a></li>\n        <li><a href=\"#skills\">Skills</a></li>\n        <li><a href=\"#contact\">Contact</a></li>\n      </ul>\n    </nav>\n  </header>\n  <main>\n    <section id=\"about\">\n      <h2>About Me</h2>\n      <p>Passionate about building intuitive software and teaching code.</p>\n    </section>\n  </main>\n  <footer>\n    <p>© 2026 Mohammed Bargisy. Built with semantic HTML5.</p>\n  </footer>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Front-End Best Practice**: Always validate your HTML tags, close all open elements, and use semantic tags (<article>, <section>, <nav>) instead of excessive generic <div> containers!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nComplete this practical challenge:\n1. **Goal**: Expand this portfolio with a skills table, an image avatar, a project showcase list, and a contact form!\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Paste the code into the HTML tab, observe the live rendered preview, and check the interactive results!\n",
