    "summary": "Discover the pros and cons of inline styles vs internal stylesheets vs external .css files.",
    "content": "# Lesson 02: Three Ways to Apply CSS: Inline, Internal <style>, and External <link>\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 02** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Three Ways to Apply CSS: Inline, Internal <style>, and External <link>**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nDiscover the pros and cons of inline styles vs internal stylesheets vs external .css files.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n<!-- External link: Recommended best practice -->\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\n\n<!-- Internal stylesheet -->\n<style>\n  h1 { color: #2563eb; }\n</style>\n\n<!-- Inline style -->\n<p style=\"color: #64748b;\">Muted text</p>\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Link an external stylesheet to your HTML page and inspect it in dev tools.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
