    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Target elements based on their exact relationships in the DOM hierarchy.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Combinators: Descendant, Child (>), Adjacent (+), and General Sibling (~)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 04** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Combinators: Descendant, Child (>), Adjacent (+), and General Sibling (~)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nTarget elements based on their exact relationships in the DOM hierarchy.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n/* Descendant: any p inside article */\narticle p { color: #334155; }\n\n/* Direct child: only immediate li of nav */\nnav > ul > li { display: inline-block; }\n\n/* Adjacent sibling: h2 immediately followed by p */\nh2 + p { font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; }\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Select only the first paragraph directly following an h2 heading using the adjacent combinator (+).\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
    "tags": "CSS, Front-End, Lesson 04, Web Design, Beginner",
