    "summary": "Understand the nuclear option in CSS specificity and how to write clean code without !important.",
    "content": "# Lesson 07: The !important Declaration & Why You Should Avoid Overusing It\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 07** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **The !important Declaration & Why You Should Avoid Overusing It**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nUnderstand the nuclear option in CSS specificity and how to write clean code without !important.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.forced-color {\n  color: #10b981 !important; /* Overrides everything */\n}\n/* Tip: Refactor specificity rather than chaining !important */\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Refactor a conflicting CSS rule without resorting to the !important rule.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
