    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Add depth and elevation to cards using multi-layer box shadows.",
    "content": "# Lesson 24: Box Shadows: Understanding Offset-X, Offset-Y, Blur, Spread, and Color\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 24** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Box Shadows: Understanding Offset-X, Offset-Y, Blur, Spread, and Color**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nAdd depth and elevation to cards using multi-layer box shadows.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.card-elevated {\n  background: white;\n  border-radius: 16px;\n  /* x=0, y=10px, blur=15px, spread=-3px, subtle dark shadow */\n  box-shadow: 0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1),\n              0 4px 6px -4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Create a card with a subtle rest shadow that elevates higher on hover.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
