    "summary": "Manage containers when child content exceeds boundaries, and create custom scrollbars.",
    "content": "# Lesson 29: The CSS Overflow Property: visible, hidden, scroll, and auto\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 29** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **The CSS Overflow Property: visible, hidden, scroll, and auto**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nManage containers when child content exceeds boundaries, and create custom scrollbars.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.scroll-box {\n  max-height: 200px;\n  overflow-y: auto;  /* Shows scrollbar only when content overflows */\n  overflow-x: hidden;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Create a fixed-height chat transcript box that scrolls smoothly when new messages exceed 300px.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
