    "summary": "Understand the normal document flow and why top, left, right, bottom have no effect on static elements.",
    "content": "# Lesson 32: CSS Positioning: static (Default Document Flow)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 32** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **CSS Positioning: static (Default Document Flow)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nUnderstand the normal document flow and why top, left, right, bottom have no effect on static elements.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.normal-flow {\n  position: static; /* Default browser positioning */\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Inspect an element in normal document flow and verify that setting top: 20px does nothing without relative/absolute positioning.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
