    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Offset elements relative to their normal position without disrupting surrounding layout.",
    "content": "# Lesson 33: CSS Positioning: relative (Nudging & Containing Context)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 33** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **CSS Positioning: relative (Nudging & Containing Context)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nOffset elements relative to their normal position without disrupting surrounding layout.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.relative-parent {\n  position: relative; /* Becomes reference anchor for absolute children */\n  top: 5px;            /* Nudge down 5px */\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Create a card with position: relative so an internal badge can position itself relative to the card's top-right corner.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
    "tags": "CSS, Front-End, Lesson 33, Web Design, Intermediate",
