    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Remove elements from normal flow and position them precisely relative to the nearest positioned ancestor.",
    "content": "# Lesson 34: CSS Positioning: absolute (Detaching from Document Flow)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 34** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **CSS Positioning: absolute (Detaching from Document Flow)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nRemove elements from normal flow and position them precisely relative to the nearest positioned ancestor.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.badge-corner {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 10px;\n  right: 10px;\n  background-color: #ef4444;\n  color: white;\n  padding: 4px 8px;\n  border-radius: 999px;\n  font-size: 11px;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Place a 'NEW' notification badge in the top-right corner of an e-commerce product card using absolute positioning.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
    "tags": "CSS, Front-End, Lesson 34, Web Design, Intermediate",
