    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Create headers and table rows that scroll normally until reaching an offset, then stick in place.",
    "content": "# Lesson 36: CSS Positioning: sticky (The Best of Relative and Fixed)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 36** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **CSS Positioning: sticky (The Best of Relative and Fixed)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nCreate headers and table rows that scroll normally until reaching an offset, then stick in place.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.sticky-navbar {\n  position: sticky;\n  top: 0;\n  z-index: 50;\n  background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);\n  backdrop-filter: blur(8px);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Implement a table header (thead tr th) with position: sticky; top: 0 so columns stay visible during long scrolls.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
