    "category_name": "Modern CSS & Design",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Control which elements appear on top and understand why a high z-index can still fail inside a lower stacking context.",
    "content": "# Lesson 37: The Stacking Context & z-index Layering\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 37** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **The Stacking Context & z-index Layering**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nControl which elements appear on top and understand why a high z-index can still fail inside a lower stacking context.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.modal-backdrop {\n  position: fixed;\n  inset: 0;\n  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);\n  z-index: 100;\n}\n.modal-window {\n  position: fixed;\n  top: 50%; left: 50%;\n  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\n  z-index: 101;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Diagnose and fix a z-index conflict where a dropdown menu was hidden behind a neighboring video banner.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
    "tags": "CSS, Front-End, Lesson 37, Web Design, Intermediate",
