    "summary": "Style interactive UI feedback during mouse hover, click press, and keyboard tab focus.",
    "content": "# Lesson 38: Pseudo-Classes Part 1: User Action States (:hover, :active, :focus, :focus-visible)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 38** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Pseudo-Classes Part 1: User Action States (:hover, :active, :focus, :focus-visible)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nStyle interactive UI feedback during mouse hover, click press, and keyboard tab focus.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n.btn {\n  background: #2563eb;\n  transition: background 0.2s;\n}\n.btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }\n.btn:active { transform: scale(0.98); }\n.btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #93c5fd; }\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Design a polished button with distinct visual states for hover, active click, and accessible keyboard focus.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
