    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Create zebra-striped tables, alternating card layouts, and formulaic selections.",
    "content": "# Lesson 40: Pseudo-Classes Part 3: The Powerful :nth-child(n) and :nth-of-type() Formulas\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 40** of the Front-End Developer CSS Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Pseudo-Classes Part 3: The Powerful :nth-child(n) and :nth-of-type() Formulas**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & Architecture\nCreate zebra-striped tables, alternating card layouts, and formulaic selections.\n\nCSS is the design language of the web. Understanding how browsers calculate styles, render layout trees, and handle modern responsive properties gives you the power to create breathtaking user experiences.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive CSS Sandbox\nReview the CSS snippet below and try it in the **Web Dev Lab**:\n\n```css\n/* Zebra striping */\ntbody tr:nth-child(odd) { background-color: #f8fafc; }\ntbody tr:nth-child(even) { background-color: #ffffff; }\n\n/* Every 3rd item */\n.grid-item:nth-child(3n) { margin-right: 0; }\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Modern CSS Best Practice**: Avoid magic numbers! Rely on responsive units (rem, em, clamp, %, fr), keep specificity low, and design mobile-first!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Style an alternating timeline where even items align right and odd items align left using :nth-child(even).\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your styles in the CSS editor tab and watch the live preview update instantly!\n",
    "tags": "CSS, Front-End, Lesson 40, Web Design, Intermediate",
