    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Understand why modern JavaScript exclusively uses let for reassignable variables and const for immutable references.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Variables: var vs let vs const & Block Scope Mechanics\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 04** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Variables: var vs let vs const & Block Scope Mechanics**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nUnderstand why modern JavaScript exclusively uses let for reassignable variables and const for immutable references.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst PI = 3.14159; // Cannot reassign!\nlet currentScore = 10;\ncurrentScore += 5;   // Valid reassignment\n\nif (true) {\n  let blockScoped = \"Hidden outside\";\n  var functionScoped = \"Leaked outside (avoid!)\";\n}\n// console.log(blockScoped); -> ReferenceError\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Demonstrate the block scoping of let versus function scoping of var inside an if-block.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
