    "summary": "Master the 7 primitive types in JavaScript and check types dynamically with typeof.",
    "content": "# Lesson 05: Primitive Data Types: String, Number, Boolean, Null, Undefined, Symbol, BigInt\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 05** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Primitive Data Types: String, Number, Boolean, Null, Undefined, Symbol, BigInt**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nMaster the 7 primitive types in JavaScript and check types dynamically with typeof.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst title = \"JavaScript Fundamentals\"; // string\nconst price = 49.99;                        // number\nconst isEnrolled = true;                     // boolean\nlet unassigned;                              // undefined\nconst emptyValue = null;                     // object (historic JS quirk!)\n\nconsole.log(typeof title);       // \"string\"\nconsole.log(typeof isEnrolled);  // \"boolean\"\nconsole.log(typeof unassigned);  // \"undefined\" \n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Declare one variable of each primitive type and log their typeof outputs.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
