    "summary": "Slice substrings cleanly using positive and negative indices and replace keywords.",
    "content": "# Lesson 13: Extracting Substrings: .slice(), .substring(), and .replace()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 13** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Extracting Substrings: .slice(), .substring(), and .replace()**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nSlice substrings cleanly using positive and negative indices and replace keywords.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst text = \"JavaScript is slow and difficult.\";\nconst corrected = text.replace(\"slow and difficult\", \"blazing fast and intuitive\");\nconsole.log(corrected);\n\nconst card = \"4111222233334444\";\nconst masked = \"****-****-****-\" + card.slice(-4);\nconsole.log(\"Masked Card:\", masked);\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Mask all characters of an email address except the first letter and domain (e.g. m***@domain.com).\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
