    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Explicitly convert data types and master truthiness rules in JavaScript.",
    "content": "# Lesson 14: Type Conversion vs Type Coercion: Number(), String(), Boolean(), and parseInt()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 14** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Type Conversion vs Type Coercion: Number(), String(), Boolean(), and parseInt()**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nExplicitly convert data types and master truthiness rules in JavaScript.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst inputYear = \"2026\";\nconst numYear = Number(inputYear); // Explicit conversion\nconst floatPrice = parseFloat(\"49.95 USD\"); // 49.95\n\nconsole.log(Boolean(\"\"));      // false (empty string is falsy)\nconsole.log(Boolean(\"Hello\")); // true (truthy)\nconsole.log(Boolean(0));       // false (falsy)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Convert a user input price string '$129.50' into a real JavaScript floating-point number.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
