    "category_name": "JavaScript Development",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Memorize the falsy values: false, 0, -0, 0n, '', null, undefined, and NaN.",
    "content": "# Lesson 15: Truthy vs Falsy: The 8 Falsy Values in JavaScript\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 15** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Truthy vs Falsy: The 8 Falsy Values in JavaScript**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nMemorize the falsy values: false, 0, -0, 0n, '', null, undefined, and NaN.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nfunction checkUser(user) {\n  if (user) {\n    console.log(`Welcome back, ${user}`);\n  } else {\n    console.log(\"No user signed in (falsy)\");\n  }\n}\ncheckUser(\"\");    // Falsy!\ncheckUser(\"Sara\");// Truthy!\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: List all 8 falsy values in JavaScript and test each inside an if-statement.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
