    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Evaluate an expression against multiple case values cleanly.",
    "content": "# Lesson 18: The switch Statement: Multi-Condition Matching with break and default\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 18** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **The switch Statement: Multi-Condition Matching with break and default**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nEvaluate an expression against multiple case values cleanly.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst command = \"start\";\n\nswitch (command) {\n  case \"start\":\n    console.log(\"🚀 Server starting...\");\n    break;\n  case \"stop\":\n    console.log(\"🛑 Server stopped.\");\n    break;\n  case \"restart\":\n    console.log(\"🔄 Rebooting...\");\n    break;\n  default:\n    console.warn(\"Unknown command received.\");\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Build a weekday planner that logs the daily meeting schedule for 'Monday' through 'Friday' using switch.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
