    "summary": "Prevent 'Cannot read property of undefined' runtime crashes with modern optional chaining.",
    "content": "# Lesson 21: Optional Chaining (?.): Safely Accessing Deeply Nested Properties\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 21** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Optional Chaining (?.): Safely Accessing Deeply Nested Properties**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nPrevent 'Cannot read property of undefined' runtime crashes with modern optional chaining.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst userProfile = {\n  id: 101,\n  name: \"Mohammed\"\n  // address is undefined!\n};\n\n// Safe access: returns undefined instead of throwing error!\nconsole.log(\"Zipcode:\", userProfile.address?.zipcode);\nconsole.log(\"City:\", userProfile.address?.city?.name ?? \"Unknown City\");\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Query a deeply nested API response object safely without throwing a TypeError.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
