    "summary": "Mutate arrays at both the end (push/pop) and beginning (unshift/shift).",
    "content": "# Lesson 28: Adding & Removing Elements: push(), pop(), unshift(), and shift()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 28** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Adding & Removing Elements: push(), pop(), unshift(), and shift()**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nMutate arrays at both the end (push/pop) and beginning (unshift/shift).\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst queue = [\"User1\", \"User2\"];\nqueue.push(\"User3\");      // Append to end\nconst first = queue.shift(); // Remove from front\nconsole.log(\"Served:\", first);\nconsole.log(\"Current queue:\", queue);\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Implement a FIFO (First-In, First-Out) job queue using push() and shift().\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
