    "summary": "Extract portions of an array without modifying the original source array.",
    "content": "# Lesson 29: Slicing Arrays (.slice()): Copying & Extracting Subarrays Non-Destructively\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 29** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Slicing Arrays (.slice()): Copying & Extracting Subarrays Non-Destructively**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nExtract portions of an array without modifying the original source array.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst colors = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\", \"yellow\", \"purple\"];\nconst primaryColors = colors.slice(0, 3);\nconsole.log(\"Extracted:\", primaryColors);\nconsole.log(\"Original untouched:\", colors);\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Extract the top 3 leaderboard winners from a 10-person race array using .slice().\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
