    "summary": "Perform powerful in-place mutations at any target index in an array.",
    "content": "# Lesson 30: Modifying in Place with .splice(): Inserting, Deleting, and Replacing\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 30** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Modifying in Place with .splice(): Inserting, Deleting, and Replacing**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nPerform powerful in-place mutations at any target index in an array.\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nconst months = [\"Jan\", \"March\", \"April\", \"June\"];\nmonths.splice(1, 0, \"Feb\"); // Insert 'Feb' at index 1 without deleting\nconsole.log(\"With Feb inserted:\", months);\n\nmonths.splice(4, 1, \"May\"); // Replace 'June' with 'May'\nconsole.log(\"Updated months:\", months);\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Remove 2 items from the middle of an array and replace them with 3 new items in one .splice() call.\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
