    "summary": "Master asynchronous data fetching and modern architecture: understanding asynchronous javascript & the event loop (call stack vs task queue).",
    "content": "# Lesson 89: Understanding Asynchronous JavaScript & The Event Loop (Call Stack vs Task Queue)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 89** of the Front-End Developer JavaScript Curriculum at Bargisy eSchool! In this lesson, you will master **Understanding Asynchronous JavaScript & The Event Loop (Call Stack vs Task Queue)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concept & JavaScript Runtime\nMaster asynchronous data fetching and modern architecture: understanding asynchronous javascript & the event loop (call stack vs task queue).\n\nJavaScript is the world's most versatile programming language, powering billions of interactive websites, web applications, and servers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive Code Implementation\nStudy and run this JavaScript code in the **Web Dev Lab** or browser console:\n\n```javascript\nasync function loadData() {\n  try {\n    console.log(\"Fetching data asynchronously...\");\n    // const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error(\"Fetch failed:\", err);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **JavaScript Pro Tip**: Always use === for strict comparisons, declare variables with const and let (never var), and catch asynchronous errors with try/catch!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. **Goal**: Implement a full asynchronous workflow demonstrating understanding asynchronous javascript & the event loop (call stack vs task queue).\n2. Open the **Interactive Web Dev Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Switch to the JS tab, run your code, and check the integrated console drawer for outputs and logs!\n",
